36
00:03:08,061 --> 00:03:13,180
The history of firearms can be charted by a continuous, ongoing series of technical

37
00:03:13,181 --> 00:03:19,004
advances. The first hand cannons replaced bows and arrows.

38
00:03:19,005 --> 00:03:22,820
Match locks replaced the hand cannons.

39
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:32,900
Then came wheel locks, followed by flint locks, cartridge revolvers, and ultimately automatic

40
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:34,152
pistols and rifles.

41
00:03:34,153 --> 00:03:39,620
In any given time, a particular gun would be the gun of the future. In other words,

42
00:03:39,621 --> 00:03:44,520
the guns of the past at their particular place and time in history were futuristic guns.

43
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:53,240
The period since World War II has seen a rapid acceleration in improvements to gun technology.

44
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:57,840
The result has been guns of the future that exist today.

45
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:06,220
In the mid-1960s, for example, the Army introduced the M16 assault rifle,

46
00:04:06,221 --> 00:04:08,940
which replaced the standard M14.

47
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:17,660
Initially called the AR-16, the M16 was made from metal alloys and plastics.

48
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:23,280
Thus the gun was much lighter, a tremendous benefit for soldiers in the field.

49
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:29,720
I was a young Marine in Vietnam at that time and carried an M14.

50
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:34,260
And the M14 had been the first gun that I had actually fired.

51
00:04:34,261 --> 00:04:40,120
I formed the opinion that the M14 was certainly perhaps one of the finest rifles ever made.

52
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:46,300
However, I think, looking back at it, and even looking at the AR-1516 at that time,

53
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:49,240
it was a lot lighter, easier to carry.

54
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,620
I think it was certainly a weapon of the future.

55
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,800
But for every success, there have been dozens of misfires.

56
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,520
Some attempts failed miserably.

57
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:10,760
Maybe you could call them solutions for problems that didn't exist, but they were kind of fun

58
00:05:10,761 --> 00:05:13,360
