223
00:20:36,618 --> 00:20:43,860
You still have the same security that you had in those positions in Vietnam, but you can precisely engage the enemy.

224
00:20:43,861 --> 00:20:52,360
However, the high-tech sighting systems of the Land Warrior go far beyond simple video technology.

225
00:20:54,120 --> 00:21:02,240
The gun is also equipped with day-for-night sights that allow a soldier to look into the darkness and see as if it were high noon.

226
00:21:05,140 --> 00:21:12,740
And to ensure that the system has every technology currently available, the Army has incorporated thermal sighting as well.

227
00:21:13,860 --> 00:21:19,420
A soldier can detect the body heat of an enemy up to 500 yards away.

228
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:25,620
Let's say you're at night or you're having trouble identifying targets in a wood line.

229
00:21:25,920 --> 00:21:34,140
You can flip over to your thermal weapon sight and the body heat of an individual brings you up like a big light bulb in a dark area.

230
00:21:35,260 --> 00:21:38,500
And again, there are crosshairs on there lined with the barrel.

231
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:43,660
And you can lay your sight directly on the target again and return fire.

232
00:21:43,860 --> 00:21:46,880
So from a personal perspective, personal engagement standpoint,

233
00:21:46,881 --> 00:21:54,100
the weapon system becomes more a part of the individual soldier

234
00:21:54,101 --> 00:21:56,280
and a more precise weapon.

235
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:02,660
Perfecting the land warrior requires sophisticated training facilities as well.

236
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:07,400
This special night vision range is a high-tech marvel unto itself.

237
00:22:07,401 --> 00:22:14,040
We have the ability to turn the lights off.

238
00:22:14,041 --> 00:22:16,800
We can go in complete darkness, as you can see.

239
00:22:17,940 --> 00:22:21,820
And we can use the image intensification devices,

240
00:22:21,980 --> 00:22:28,100
which depend upon a small amount of light to intensify and provide you an image.

241
00:22:31,980 --> 00:22:35,640
Though the land warrior system is still in the prototype stage,

242
00:22:35,641 --> 00:22:39,820
the Army hopes it will be ready for the field within two years.

243
00:22:42,260 --> 00:22:48,200
The challenge now is to make the components of the Land Warrior system even smaller and lighter.

244
00:22:48,920 --> 00:22:52,660
The total package now weighs less than 25 pounds.

245
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:56,080
On this system right here, since it's a demonstrator model and everything,

246
00:22:56,600 --> 00:22:59,640
what we've done is we've incorporated the soldier intercom system here,

247
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:01,120
which this will be done away with.

248
00:23:01,121 --> 00:23:06,120
This system right here is the DCM, which basically runs from the IHAS through to the computer system.

249
00:23:06,121 --> 00:23:10,120
This will be reduced about half its size.

250
00:23:10,121 --> 00:23:15,120
The cable itself, we're still working the routing aspect of it and which direction we want to go with it.

251
00:23:15,121 --> 00:23:21,120
One of the main drawbacks to the system is a lack of battery time.

252
00:23:21,121 --> 00:23:28,120
Currently, all the computer technology in a Land Warrior drains a battery in about 12 hours.

253
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:37,340
A soldier can only carry two of the batteries, so he must be resupplied every 24 hours.

254
00:23:38,040 --> 00:23:40,200
The other factor is cost.

255
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:44,600
So far, millions have been spent developing the land warrior system,

256
00:23:45,080 --> 00:23:49,560
and millions more are necessary to get it into the hands of soldiers in the field.

257
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:53,120
They're going to be more expensive.

258
00:23:53,880 --> 00:23:55,740
There's going to be more things that can go wrong.

259
00:23:55,741 --> 00:24:00,200
and it's going to take a greater degree of training and sophistication

260
00:24:00,201 --> 00:24:01,940
to get the average soldier to use these things.

261
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:05,320
I've been in the Army and I can remember getting the average recruit

262
00:24:05,321 --> 00:24:08,140
just to be able to work his M-14 properly was difficult.

263
00:24:10,120 --> 00:24:14,760
But Army officials say soldiers trained with a land warrior pick it up quickly.

264
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:19,760
For young people who grew up in the computer age,

265
00:24:19,761 --> 00:24:23,180
having a mouse control button on a gun seems natural.

266
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:36,800
The Army believes that the new Land Warrior system is the weapon of the future, not just because of its firing capabilities, but as part of a total information system.

267
00:24:39,940 --> 00:24:51,060
Many envision a day when a soldier looking at an enemy on the ground can send information or an image to a pilot in a fighter jet high above the combat zone.

268
00:24:51,061 --> 00:24:58,940
In essence, this gun of the future will fight on a virtual battlefield.

269
00:25:02,280 --> 00:25:03,800
Warfare is managed chaos.

270
00:25:04,580 --> 00:25:09,280
And what you want to do is you want to allow the enemy to have much more of the chaos

271
00:25:09,281 --> 00:25:10,400
and you more of the management.

272
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:13,840
So you want to make sure that as the pilot comes in,

273
00:25:13,900 --> 00:25:16,080
he can discern where you are, where the enemy is,

274
00:25:16,140 --> 00:25:18,240
he understands what his targets are,

275
00:25:18,241 --> 00:25:22,800
And so you want him to gather data from the soldiers on the ground.

276
00:25:23,780 --> 00:25:27,200
But not every supergun is geared for killing an enemy.

277
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:32,960
In the gritty world of police work, the objective is often the opposite.

278
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:38,520
For the deputies in the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department,

279
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:43,680
using modern technology in their weapons may help them with the toughest part of their job,

280
00:25:44,060 --> 00:25:46,760
fighting criminals and saving lives.

281
00:25:48,240 --> 00:25:54,960
To be accepted by the Army, the land warrior system must weigh less than 80 pounds.

282
00:25:54,961 --> 00:26:02,680
Currently, the personal load weighs 79 pounds, while the combat load weighs 92 pounds, including water and ammunition.

283
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:05,460
Super Guns will return in a moment.

284
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:11,960
We now return to Super Guns on Modern Marvels.

285
00:26:11,961 --> 00:26:22,700
For police officers across the country, taking a human life is a last desperate resort.

286
00:26:25,940 --> 00:26:29,720
If the only tool you have is a hammer, you tend to think of every problem as a nail.

287
00:26:30,180 --> 00:26:34,160
And as a result of that, if the only way you have of resolving this problem is with lethal force,

288
00:26:34,360 --> 00:26:36,820
you tend to think of it as a lethal force problem.

289
00:26:37,240 --> 00:26:39,680
Well, there's some real problems with that.

290
00:26:39,681 --> 00:26:45,100
One of which is that it takes substantial provocation to use lethal force.

291
00:26:45,660 --> 00:26:49,540
And right along with that provocation comes a substantial amount of risk.

292
00:26:50,180 --> 00:26:56,360
So anything that we can do to intervene at a lower level by using less force or less lethal force

293
00:26:56,361 --> 00:27:02,140
allows the officer to resolve the situation without the accompanying risk.

294
00:27:02,141 --> 00:27:12,380
I think there's a trend that maybe to be more tolerant, to find techniques, maybe to use less lethal force.

295
00:27:12,381 --> 00:27:21,160
I think that police officers 10, 15, 20 years from now need to be a lot smarter than we've been in the last, through the 60s, 70s and 80s.

296
00:27:21,460 --> 00:27:22,420
Certainly more tolerant.

297
00:27:24,300 --> 00:27:31,580
But developing non-lethal methods to fight criminals, many of whom are armed to the teeth, is a speculative venture.

298
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:36,380
There's such a new field that some of these terms we've actually had to coin,

299
00:27:36,820 --> 00:27:38,400
non-lethal being one of them.

300
00:27:38,860 --> 00:27:40,980
There was never a way to describe that.

301
00:27:41,500 --> 00:27:46,380
What happens is that a less lethal weapon, for the first time in history,

302
00:27:46,660 --> 00:27:49,820
is being defined by its intent and not its capability.

303
00:27:50,360 --> 00:27:54,700
Where a lethal weapon attempts to defeat an adversary's ability to fight,

304
00:27:54,701 --> 00:27:57,980
a less lethal weapon attempts to defeat his will to fight.

305
00:27:57,981 --> 00:28:05,600
Finding clever ways not to kill people who are fighting the police has involved years of trial and error.

306
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:16,840
Rubber bullets, meant to be used in riot situations to strike a person in the knees, can ricochet off the ground and shatter a windpipe.

307
00:28:20,040 --> 00:28:23,260
There's two misnomers with less lethal force.

308
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:27,140
One is that it won't kill you, and that's not true in most cases.

309
00:28:27,141 --> 00:28:30,180
and the other one is that it's painless, and that's definitely not true.

310
00:28:30,420 --> 00:28:33,360
To more precisely define it and keep it in the minds of our deputies,

311
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:37,040
we tell them that it's less lethal or less than lethal,

312
00:28:37,220 --> 00:28:42,380
meaning that we're trying to intervene without having to resort to our sidearms.

313
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:50,940
At the L.A. County Sheriff's firing range,

314
00:28:50,941 --> 00:28:54,500
a new generation of nontraditional weapons holds promise.

315
00:28:57,140 --> 00:29:03,360
What has happened is that in the last four or five years, the Department of Defense has been downsizing.

316
00:29:03,361 --> 00:29:08,360
And as a result of that, the developers and defense contractors and federal laboratories

317
00:29:08,361 --> 00:29:17,160
who have had this huge defense market in the past have had to find some way or some other place to sell their goods.

318
00:29:17,280 --> 00:29:21,880
And as a result of that, very similar in application and function is law enforcement.

319
00:29:21,881 --> 00:29:26,880
The premise is simple.

320
00:29:27,560 --> 00:29:31,240
Stop a violent criminal without having to take that person's life.

321
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:35,460
Police believe the best way to do that is to inflict pain.

322
00:29:38,360 --> 00:29:40,940
The idea of pain we're comfortable with.

323
00:29:41,040 --> 00:29:42,760
We can use pain to induce compliance.

324
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:48,900
The problem is that most of these devices also have trauma accompanied with the pain.

325
00:29:49,160 --> 00:29:50,240
Pain is okay.

326
00:29:50,241 --> 00:29:51,880
the trauma we're trying to avoid.

327
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:55,440
It's usually something that we're willing to live with for right now,

328
00:29:55,900 --> 00:29:59,560
bruising, abrasions, but sometimes it can break bones,

329
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:01,080
it can leave serious bruises,

330
00:30:01,580 --> 00:30:04,740
and depending on how it's used, serious injuries,

331
00:30:04,840 --> 00:30:06,460
internal injuries and things like that.

332
00:30:09,040 --> 00:30:11,480
To try and avoid inflicting trauma,

333
00:30:12,220 --> 00:30:14,560
sheriffs are using an arsenal of new weapons

334
00:30:14,561 --> 00:30:17,300
in their battle-to-end officer-involved shootings.

335
00:30:17,301 --> 00:30:25,300
Though this weapon looks like a regular shotgun, it actually fires shells filled with beanbags.

336
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:30,300
This is probably the boldest venture that we've taken so far.

337
00:30:30,301 --> 00:30:34,300
Every radio car has one in the field right now, keeps it in the trunk.

338
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:36,300
It's available when they need it.

339
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:38,300
We've saved I don't even know how many people's lives.

340
00:30:38,301 --> 00:30:43,300
The very first weekend that we employed it, we saved a guy's life that had a knife.

341
00:30:43,301 --> 00:30:47,860
Had he, had that same incident occurred even a day before, they'd have had to kill him.

342
00:30:50,220 --> 00:30:55,280
Little training is needed because the stun bag weapon works just like any shotgun.

343
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:05,600
The biggest problem with a bean bag right now is that it's not very aerodynamic.

344
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:07,700
It doesn't fly straight.

345
00:31:07,860 --> 00:31:10,480
And as a result of that, it doesn't go where we want it to go.

346
00:31:10,481 --> 00:31:15,240
They're either square or circular pads that are curled up inside the shotgun shell,

347
00:31:15,460 --> 00:31:18,820
and then they come out of the tube of the shotgun and then open up.

348
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,360
In order to be effective, they need to strike flat.

349
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:25,460
But in order to fly, they need to allow the wind to go around them,

350
00:31:25,500 --> 00:31:27,000
so they fly like a leaf in the wind.

351
00:31:29,100 --> 00:31:33,780
A step up on the pain threshold is a severe-looking weapon called the Arwen.

352
00:31:34,620 --> 00:31:39,500
The gun fires 5-inch shells of hardened plastic that can dent a metal door.

353
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:54,480
As you can see by the impacts on this metal door, that the velocity and the weight is

354
00:31:54,760 --> 00:32:00,860
enough to give somebody no question that they've been hit.

355
00:32:00,861 --> 00:32:04,260
And generally one or two shots puts somebody down.

356
00:32:04,261 --> 00:32:05,460
We saved a woman's life.

357
00:32:05,461 --> 00:32:08,040
She was going to commit suicide.

358
00:32:08,041 --> 00:32:13,461
had a .357 underneath her chin, and we hit her right in the arm with the R1.

359
00:32:13,462 --> 00:32:14,460
Broke her arm,

360
00:32:14,960 --> 00:32:18,919
and we felt bad. We thought, oh, we're really going to catch heat for this.

361
00:32:18,920 --> 00:32:20,080
But we were the heroes

362
00:32:20,081 --> 00:32:25,040
because the alternative to that same situation was to shoot her or let her shoot herself.

363
00:32:25,660 --> 00:32:29,480
And so as a result of that, even though it wasn't the circumstance that we had hoped for,

364
00:32:29,740 --> 00:32:31,753
it was so much better than the alternative.

365
00:32:31,754 --> 00:32:34,000
And that was one of the times that we coined the

366
00:32:34,001 --> 00:32:37,720
phrase, the standard is not perfection. The standard is the alternative.

367
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:47,060
A crucial factor in implementing less than lethal force is keeping the police far away

368
00:32:47,061 --> 00:32:49,191
from an armed suspect.

369
00:32:49,192 --> 00:32:55,680
One answer is firing thick plastic rings from an air-powered rifle.

370
00:32:58,600 --> 00:33:02,800
The rings can travel up to 100 yards and still be effective.

371
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:16,640
When used properly, the ring can knock the wind out of a suspect

372
00:33:16,641 --> 00:33:20,120
or inflict enough pain to stop a person in their tracks.

373
00:33:24,220 --> 00:33:29,480
If all else fails, police are working on a less high-tech, non-lethal weapon

374
00:33:29,481 --> 00:33:34,160
that utilizes an ammunition typically associated with teenage pranksters.

375
00:33:35,440 --> 00:33:37,820
This gun fires stink bombs,

376
00:33:38,560 --> 00:33:41,140
plastic pellets filled with a revolutionary liquid

377
00:33:41,141 --> 00:33:44,440
that smells worse than just about anything on Earth.

378
00:33:45,560 --> 00:33:48,120
It smells like a combination of fecal matter,

379
00:33:48,900 --> 00:33:52,180
dead mammals, garlic and sulfur all mixed together.

380
00:33:56,840 --> 00:33:58,900
You just wave it under your nose.

381
00:33:59,480 --> 00:34:07,480
This stuff will gag a maggot.

382
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:09,520
This stuff is going to be great.

383
00:34:10,220 --> 00:34:12,760
We don't even know everything we can do with this stuff yet.

384
00:34:14,220 --> 00:34:17,320
Another version fires pellets filled with pepper powder,

385
00:34:17,860 --> 00:34:21,180
a choking compound that irritates the eyes and throat.

386
00:34:21,181 --> 00:34:34,420
So we have special masks for the deputies that filter out the powder, but also on the

387
00:34:34,421 --> 00:34:40,060
suspect's clothing, we need to keep it on him so we don't contaminate our car, contaminate

388
00:34:40,061 --> 00:34:40,761
our jail.

389
00:34:40,762 --> 00:34:47,340
So what we've come up with is conventional, regular hairspray that we spray it on and

390
00:34:47,341 --> 00:34:49,124
it seals it to the close.

391
00:34:49,125 --> 00:34:54,120
So this powder that's here will be sealed on there with the stickiness

392
00:34:54,121 --> 00:34:55,535
of the hairspray.

393
00:34:55,536 --> 00:35:02,360
For the police, attempts to put weapons with less than lethal force into the field

394
00:35:02,361 --> 00:35:05,800
is essential to the future of law enforcement.

395
00:35:05,801 --> 00:35:09,540
Because when an officer is forced to fire his gun,

396
00:35:09,541 --> 00:35:12,940
the repercussions for everyone involved are severe.

397
00:35:12,941 --> 00:35:22,020
There is some tremendous trauma, particularly psychological trauma, that accompany people that use lethal force.

398
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:25,440
We send our people to a psychologist for five days.

399
00:35:26,040 --> 00:35:27,340
They're taken out of the field.

400
00:35:27,520 --> 00:35:34,500
I mean, it's a tremendous, life-changing event when you're put in a position that you're not allowed to flee from

401
00:35:34,501 --> 00:35:38,200
and have to resolve and end up having to kill someone.

402
00:35:38,201 --> 00:35:44,420
So less lethal provide that option of intervening without that accompanying humanitarian problems.

403
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:48,440
Not to say that there's none there with it, it's just not to the degree with lethal force.

404
00:35:50,760 --> 00:35:55,660
Implementing less than lethal weaponry on a wide scale may still be years away.

405
00:35:56,800 --> 00:36:04,340
In the meantime, gun manufacturers are looking for ways to make traditional weapons not only more effective, but safer.

406
00:36:04,341 --> 00:36:11,340
Thanks to computer technology, these guns may someday have brains as well as bullets.

407
00:36:14,340 --> 00:36:24,985
In 1995, special forces units in Somalia considered using lasers that could temporarily blind the enemy, but thought the risk of permanent blindness was too high and

408
00:36:24,986 --> 00:36:26,340
decided against them.

409
00:36:26,341 --> 00:36:29,340
Super guns will continue in a moment.

410
00:36:29,341 --> 00:36:38,340
in a moment. We now return to Super Guns on Modern Marvels.

411
00:36:41,340 --> 00:36:48,340
Inside the grip of this Colt 45 may lie the future of Super Gun technology.

412
00:36:48,341 --> 00:36:59,380
The smart gun is equipped with a small computer, which allows the trigger to be activated only

413
00:36:59,381 --> 00:37:02,460
by its owner.

414
00:37:02,461 --> 00:37:06,840
This is possible because the shooter wears a special wristwatch programmed to restrict

415
00:37:06,841 --> 00:37:09,700
access to the gun's firing mechanism.

416
00:37:09,701 --> 00:37:18,560
If the concept proved successful, it could revolutionize guns and gun safety.

417
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:24,680
It's not as easy as putting a lock on it like some people may want to tell you.

418
00:37:25,160 --> 00:37:29,500
I think it's a situation that if we can build a better mousetrap, to coin that phrase,

419
00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:32,300
we're going to be a company to go into this next millennium

420
00:37:32,301 --> 00:37:35,440
and be the head of the game like our founder did when he first started it.

421
00:37:35,441 --> 00:37:40,940
Two years ago, after a series of highly publicized school shootings,

422
00:37:41,600 --> 00:37:47,740
officials at Colt Manufacturing decided they wanted to do something to make guns safer, much safer.

423
00:37:50,480 --> 00:37:56,640
Another motivation was the fact that 12% of the police officers killed in the line of duty each year

424
00:37:56,641 --> 00:38:00,800
are shot with their own guns, taken away from them by criminals.

425
00:38:05,440 --> 00:38:13,440
Colt hired a team of Silicon Valley designers to blend the latest in computer technology with the firing system of an automatic pistol.

426
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:28,440
This product has an electronic device that is installed in it that prevents the trigger from operating unless the weapon is in the hands of an authorized user.

427
00:38:28,441 --> 00:38:35,520
This particular weapon has been developed for the law enforcement community.

428
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:42,860
It is our second prototype of the smart gun, and it uses a transmitter.

429
00:38:43,420 --> 00:38:51,020
As you can see, the trigger is not active right now in that when you pull it, the hammer does not fire.

430
00:38:51,021 --> 00:39:00,120
However, when in the close proximity of a transmitting device, the trigger unlocks and now the gun can be fired.

431
00:39:00,560 --> 00:39:09,860
And as long as the weapon is in the hands or in close proximity to the transmitting device, the weapon becomes or stays active.

432
00:39:09,861 --> 00:39:14,600
If the weapon is taken away from the police officer or the person using it,

433
00:39:15,080 --> 00:39:19,180
such that it is no longer in proximity of the transmitter,

434
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:22,720
the weapon no longer is functional.

435
00:39:24,520 --> 00:39:29,880
The smart gun has come a long way since the first prototype was built in early 1999.

436
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:35,900
The technology that makes it work used to be housed in a large box.

437
00:39:36,220 --> 00:39:38,800
Now, it fits into the grip of the gun.

438
00:39:39,860 --> 00:39:48,960
In June 1999, our cameras were the first to record Colt's working smart gun prototype

439
00:39:48,961 --> 00:39:51,980
in action at the company's range in Hartford, Connecticut.

440
00:39:58,600 --> 00:40:03,200
Since members of law enforcement will most likely be the first to use the weapon,

441
00:40:03,920 --> 00:40:08,020
officials at Colt want to be very sure that it will work every time.

442
00:40:08,021 --> 00:40:12,516
The question now is can we do it reliably?

443
00:40:12,517 --> 00:40:21,400
And therein lies the $64,000 question and the unknowns associated with the product.

444
00:40:21,401 --> 00:40:30,100
There are a number of people that have commented that this technology is ready for prime time right now.

445
00:40:30,760 --> 00:40:32,320
That's not quite so.

446
00:40:33,820 --> 00:40:41,900
We have the technology to make the firearm functional, as you can see from this one here.

447
00:40:41,901 --> 00:40:51,200
Now the question is, is this gun going to operate reliably in the field first time, every time, for as long as the law enforcement official needs it?

448
00:40:51,400 --> 00:41:03,237
It's that reliance on computers that has many in the gun world wondering just how far technology should go in trying to improve something that has been around for more

449
00:41:03,238 --> 00:41:04,300
than 500 years.

450
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:08,460
Computers are certainly the wave of the future.

451
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:12,640
They're being included in everything, automobiles, airplanes, and firearms.

452
00:41:12,641 --> 00:41:19,775
The problem with computers, and I think we've probably all experienced this with our cars as well, is that the more things you've got going on in there, the more

453
00:41:19,776 --> 00:41:21,680
chances you have for something to go wrong.

454
00:41:21,740 --> 00:41:27,740
And when you're in a battlefield situation, you want things to work as simply and as effectively as possible.

455
00:41:28,180 --> 00:41:34,180
I am certainly no expert on electronics and computers, and I'm sure that they're probably coming up with some wonderful, wonderful systems.

456
00:41:34,181 --> 00:41:40,160
But we must always remember that there must be some simple backup system so that if something happens to these things,

457
00:41:40,320 --> 00:41:43,840
you can still fire the gun without having to worry about wearing a helmet or a heads-up display

458
00:41:43,841 --> 00:41:49,180
or some sort of a smart system whereby nobody else can fire the gun but you.

459
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:56,880
But in an age where the computer is the driving force behind nearly every new technology developed,

460
00:41:57,340 --> 00:42:02,420
there is no denying that the interaction between the microchip and the firearm is inevitable.

461
00:42:04,180 --> 00:42:13,040
I think we'll see some kind of a marriage or combination, computerized ammo, computerized guns, that will be really doing very smart things.

462
00:42:13,100 --> 00:42:18,540
It will take all the thinking other than the decision to use deadly force.

463
00:42:18,780 --> 00:42:24,860
I think those computers will do the distance, and we're already there in a sense, but I think they'll refine that even more.

464
00:42:26,260 --> 00:42:30,340
I think the marriage between the computer, the gun, and the ammo will be even greater.

465
00:42:30,341 --> 00:42:39,340
And as technology changes the world of guns, it also changes who buys guns and why.

466
00:42:40,760 --> 00:42:45,260
The person buying and carrying guns today are much different than even 30 years ago.

467
00:42:46,100 --> 00:42:47,400
You know, we grew up with firearms.

468
00:42:47,720 --> 00:42:51,760
Kids that were in high school in the 70s, you know, we hunt, we fished, we did a lot of shooting.

469
00:42:52,280 --> 00:42:56,080
Today, people are less exposed to firearms due to the political climate,

470
00:42:56,320 --> 00:42:59,940
lack of ranges to go shoot, and a general lack of hunting in this country.

471
00:42:59,941 --> 00:43:11,079
So the guns of the future have to have systems built into them, very much like cars, electric dryers for your hair, any kind of tool or appliance that's used by a

472
00:43:11,080 --> 00:43:11,780
novice.

473
00:43:12,020 --> 00:43:16,200
But with a firearm, keeping your finger off the trigger is only part of the system.

474
00:43:17,480 --> 00:43:19,260
Internally, it has to be drop-proof.

475
00:43:19,760 --> 00:43:23,280
It has to be able to function correctly at all times.

476
00:43:23,281 --> 00:43:27,920
If we wanted to just sit back and just sell guns for the sake of selling guns,

477
00:43:28,120 --> 00:43:30,740
we could write our current designs for probably another 50 years.

478
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:35,560
But we would be less responsive as a company, I feel, if we just did that.

479
00:43:37,580 --> 00:43:40,220
What will the super gun of the future be like?

480
00:43:40,820 --> 00:43:45,060
Just looking at the changes that took place with technology in the 20th century,

481
00:43:45,600 --> 00:43:46,940
it's anyone's guess.

482
00:43:47,840 --> 00:43:51,680
There's a kid growing up and reading comic books, for instance,

483
00:43:51,681 --> 00:43:54,680
and I'm reading all the crime fighters

484
00:43:54,681 --> 00:43:58,260
or I'm reading comic books about people going to the moon

485
00:43:58,261 --> 00:44:01,060
and in rocket ships and space platforms and everything

486
00:44:01,061 --> 00:44:05,080
and whoever would have thought that in the 90s or the 80s

487
00:44:05,081 --> 00:44:07,460
putting a space shuttle up is just, you know,

488
00:44:07,900 --> 00:44:10,460
a thing that can be done any day of the week.

489
00:44:11,020 --> 00:44:12,860
And so based on that experience,

490
00:44:13,120 --> 00:44:15,780
I think that 30, 40, 50 years,

491
00:44:15,880 --> 00:44:19,200
maybe we'll be using ray guns, magic things

492
00:44:19,201 --> 00:44:21,260
that won't have any failures,

493
00:44:21,261 --> 00:44:22,820
that won't shoot the wrong people.

